WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.456
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.756 --> 00:00:16.459
Unit twenty-six: Markets and Morality. In this unit, you will debate the moral limits of markets.

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.962
moral economy.

5
00:00:19.662 --> 00:00:24.413
moral economy means an economy guided by shared ideas of fairness.

6
00:00:24.713 --> 00:00:29.487
In a moral economy, prices reflect community norms.

7
00:00:30.187 --> 00:00:32.724
Your turn. Say: moral economy.

8
00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:36.657
moral economy.

9
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.336
commodification of life.

10
00:00:40.036 --> 00:00:46.598
commodification of life means treating aspects of life, such as health or relationships, as products.

11
00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:49.954
Paid queuing illustrates the commodification of life.

12
00:00:50.654 --> 00:00:54.653
Your turn. Say: commodification of life.

13
00:00:57.353 --> 00:00:59.318
commodification of life.

14
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:01.991
market fundamentalism.

15
00:01:02.691 --> 00:01:08.104
market fundamentalism means the belief that free markets solve almost every problem.

16
00:01:08.404 --> 00:01:13.202
Critics blame market fundamentalism for the 2008 crisis.

17
00:01:13.902 --> 00:01:17.436
Your turn. Say: market fundamentalism.

18
00:01:20.136 --> 00:01:21.857
market fundamentalism.

19
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.176
externality.

20
00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:31.044
externality means a cost or benefit affecting others who did not choose it.

21
00:01:31.344 --> 00:01:35.131
Pollution is a classic negative externality.

22
00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:39.076
Your turn. Say: externality.

23
00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:42.974
externality.

24
00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:45.042
public good.

25
00:01:45.742 --> 00:01:50.970
public good means something that benefits everyone and cannot easily be sold.

26
00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.223
Clean air is a public good.

27
00:01:53.923 --> 00:01:56.889
Your turn. Say: public good.

28
00:01:59.589 --> 00:02:00.671
public good.

29
00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:03.335
tragedy of the commons.

30
00:02:04.035 --> 00:02:11.073
tragedy of the commons means overuse of shared resources because individuals act in self-interest.

31
00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:13.953
Overfishing is a tragedy of the commons.

32
00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:18.037
Your turn. Say: tragedy of the commons.

33
00:02:20.737 --> 00:02:22.655
tragedy of the commons.

34
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.976
market failure.

35
00:02:25.676 --> 00:02:31.461
market failure means a situation where markets do not produce efficient or fair results.

36
00:02:31.761 --> 00:02:35.142
Climate change is the greatest market failure in history.

37
00:02:35.842 --> 00:02:38.970
Your turn. Say: market failure.

38
00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.985
market failure.

39
00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:45.298
rent-seeking.

40
00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:51.794
rent-seeking means gaining wealth by manipulating rules rather than creating value.

41
00:02:52.094 --> 00:02:55.440
Lobbying for special privileges is rent-seeking.

42
00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.373
Your turn. Say: rent-seeking.

43
00:03:02.073 --> 00:03:03.295
rent-seeking.

44
00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.643
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.943 --> 00:03:09.976
Ms. Sarah, are there things money shouldn't buy?

46
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:15.268
Michael Sandel's book on the moral limits of markets argues that there are.

47
00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:19.349
Like paying people to stand in queues for you?

48
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:27.250
Exactly. The steady commodification of everyday life, Sandel says, corrupts certain values.

49
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.178
But markets are efficient.

50
00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:37.628
Often, yes, but pollution, an externality nobody pays for, shows their limits.

51
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:40.707
And shared fishing grounds?

52
00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:47.720
A classic tragedy of the commons, the kind of market failure that requires collective rules.

53
00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:53.628
Grammar focus: Complex noun phrases (extended).

54
00:03:53.928 --> 00:03:56.369
Complex noun phrases add precision.

55
00:03:56.669 --> 00:04:02.329
Sandel's book on the moral limits of markets. The steady commodification of everyday life.

56
00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:08.959
Pollution, an externality nobody pays for, shows the limits of markets.

57
00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:14.962
Long noun phrases are powerful in writing; in speech, break them up for clarity.

58
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.020
Now read and listen. What Money Can't Buy.

59
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.979
Markets are among humanity's most powerful tools for creating wealth.

60
00:04:25.283 --> 00:04:31.300
Yet philosopher Michael Sandel warns of a shift from having a market economy to being a market society.

61
00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:39.041
In such a society, the commodification of life extends to education, health and even friendship.

62
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:47.980
Economists recognise that markets can fail, particularly where externalities such as pollution are involved.

63
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:55.509
Public goods, like clean air, are under-provided because no one can easily profit from them.

64
00:04:56.817 --> 00:05:02.891
The tragedy of the commons describes how shared resources are depleted when everyone acts alone.

65
00:05:04.183 --> 00:05:09.828
Rent-seeking, meanwhile, rewards manipulation of rules rather than genuine productivity.

66
00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:16.228
The question, then, is not whether to have markets, but where their moral limits should lie.

67
00:05:17.517 --> 00:05:20.030
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.862
A cost affecting others who did not choose it is an blank.

69
00:05:24.162 --> 00:05:29.568
A, externality. B, public good. C, rent-seeking.

70
00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.430
The answer is A: externality.

71
00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:39.986
Which is a complex noun phrase?

72
00:05:40.286 --> 00:05:45.611
A, the book. B, the controversial book on market ethics. C, books.

73
00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:53.706
The answer is B: the controversial book on market ethics.

74
00:05:55.217 --> 00:05:59.782
Clean air, a public good blank everyone, is under threat.

75
00:06:00.082 --> 00:06:04.653
A, benefits. B, benefiting. C, benefited.

76
00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.986
The answer is B: benefiting.

77
00:06:12.483 --> 00:06:16.700
Overuse of shared resources through self-interest is the blank.

78
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.521
A, tragedy of the commons. B, moral economy. C, market fundamentalism.

79
00:06:27.517 --> 00:06:30.863
The answer is A: tragedy of the commons.

80
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.673
According to Sandel, what are we shifting towards?

81
00:06:35.973 --> 00:06:41.159
A, a market society. B, a moral economy. C, degrowth.

82
00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.171
The answer is A: a market society.

83
00:06:49.683 --> 00:06:54.948
Now it's your turn. Part one: Are there things that money should not be able to buy?

84
00:07:00.248 --> 00:07:06.950
Part two: Use a complex noun phrase; give two examples and explain the principle.

85
00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:25.703
Yes. The sale of school places to the highest bidder and the buying of votes both corrupt values that depend on fairness. The principle is that some goods lose their meaning when they become commodities.

86
00:07:26.003 --> 00:07:27.968
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:28.983 --> 00:07:34.341
Great work today! You learned eight new words and complex noun phrases (extended).

88
00:07:34.641 --> 00:07:38.059
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:38.359 --> 00:07:42.324
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:42.624 --> 00:07:46.878
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

